#  Azure CLI (Command Line Interface)

## 1. Why Use Azure CLI?

The Azure portal is great for learning and one-off tasks, but Azure CLI becomes essential for real-world DevOps work.

### When CLI beats the portal

| Scenario | Portal | CLI |
|---|---|---|
| Create one VM | ✅ Fine | ✅ Fine |
| Create 10 VMs with identical config | ❌ Slow, error-prone | ✅ One script, reusable |
| Quick audit — how many VMs in a project? | ❌ Navigate multiple pages | ✅ One command |
| Share resource creation steps with team | ❌ Screenshots/docs | ✅ Commit script to Git |
| Onboard a new team member | ❌ Manual walkthrough | ✅ Clone repo, run script |
| Reduce configuration errors | ❌ Manual = human error | ✅ Script is consistent every time |

### Core advantages

- **Quick actions** — query, create, or delete resources in seconds without navigating the UI.
- **Automation** — script repetitive tasks; store in Git and share across the team.
- **Reduced errors** — a tested script produces the same result every time, eliminating manual mistakes like selecting the wrong image or missing a required field.
- **Team collaboration** — scripts in a Git repository serve as living documentation of how infrastructure is provisioned.

> **Other automation options:** ARM Templates, Bicep, Terraform — these are covered in later sessions. Azure CLI is the starting point before moving to full infrastructure-as-code.

---

## 2. Installing Azure CLI

### Windows

1. Go to the [Azure CLI documentation](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/cli/azure/install-azure-cli-windows).
2. Download the **64-bit MSI installer** (recommended over PowerShell method for compatibility).
3. Run the `.msi` file → accept the license → click **Next** through the wizard.
4. Verify installation in PowerShell:

```powershell
az version
```

> On Windows, use **PowerShell** (not Git Bash) for Azure CLI — Microsoft provides excellent PowerShell support for Azure.

### macOS

```bash
brew update && brew install azure-cli
```

Verify:

```bash
az version
```

### Linux (Ubuntu / Debian)

```bash
curl -sL https://aka.ms/InstallAzureCLIDeb | sudo bash
```

Verify:

```bash
az version
```

**Expected output (example):**

```json
{
  "azure-cli": "2.x.x",
  "azure-cli-core": "2.x.x",
  "azure-cli-telemetry": "1.x.x",
  "extensions": {}
}
```

> If `az version` returns this output, Azure CLI is correctly installed.

---

## 3. Configuring Azure CLI

After installation, link the CLI to my Azure account:

```bash
az login
```

- A browser window opens automatically.
- Sign in with my Azure account credentials.
- On success, the terminal displays a confirmation and the active subscription details.

> The CLI automatically picks up my subscription after login. All subsequent commands run against this subscription — no need to specify it separately each time.

---

## 4. CLI Command Reference — Where to Find Commands

Azure CLI documentation provides an **A–Z article list** covering every resource type.

**URL:** [https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/cli/azure/reference-index](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/cli/azure/reference-index)

**How to find a command:**
1. Open the A–Z article list.
2. Press `Ctrl+F` / `Cmd+F` and search for the resource (e.g., `az vm`, `az group`, `az storage`).
3. Click the relevant article for the full command syntax and examples.

> ⚠️ **Learn the portal first.** Azure CLI automation only makes sense when I understand the resource dependencies (e.g., a VM requires a Resource Group and VNet to already exist). Jumping to CLI without portal knowledge leads to confusing errors.

---

## 5. Create a Resource Group

Resource Group must be created before any other resource.

```bash
az group create \
  --name learn-azure-cli \
  --location eastus
```

**Verify in the portal:** Navigate to **Resource Groups** — `learn-azure-cli` should appear.

**Common parameters:**

| Parameter | Description |
|---|---|
| `--name` | Name of the Resource Group |
| `--location` | Azure region (e.g., `eastus`, `centralindia`, `westeurope`) |

---

## 6. Create a Virtual Network and Virtual Machine

The following command creates a VM **and** its VNet, subnet, public IP, and NSG in a single step:

```bash
az vm create \
  --resource-group learn-azure-cli \
  --name test-cli-vm \
  --image Ubuntu2204 \
  --vnet-name test-cli-vnet \
  --subnet example-subnet \
  --generate-ssh-keys
```

**What `--generate-ssh-keys` does:**
- If SSH keys already exist on the machine (at `~/.ssh/id_rsa`), it reuses them.
- If no keys exist (common on Windows), it generates a new key pair and saves them.
- The VM is configured with the public key; the private key is used to SSH in.

**Resources automatically created by this command:**

| Resource | Name |
|---|---|
| Virtual Network | `test-cli-vnet` |
| Subnet | `example-subnet` |
| Public IP | auto-named |
| NSG | auto-named |
| Virtual Machine | `test-cli-vm` |

**Verify in the portal:** Go to **Resource Groups** → `learn-azure-cli` — all resources should be listed.

---

## 7. Create a Storage Account

```bash
# If the Resource Group was deleted, recreate it first:
az group create \
  --name learn-azure-cli \
  --location eastus

# Create the storage account:
az storage account create \
  --name <unique-storage-account-name> \
  --resource-group learn-azure-cli \
  --location eastus \
  --sku Standard_LRS
```

> Storage account names must be **globally unique**, lowercase, 3–24 characters, letters and numbers only.

**Common `--sku` options:**

| SKU | Description |
|---|---|
| `Standard_LRS` | Locally Redundant Storage (cheapest, good for dev) |
| `Standard_GRS` | Geo-Redundant Storage (replicates to secondary region) |
| `Premium_LRS` | Premium performance (SSDs) |

---

## 8. Delete Resources

### Delete all resources by deleting the Resource Group

```bash
az group delete \
  --name learn-azure-cli
```

The CLI prompts for confirmation. Type `y` and press Enter.

### Delete without waiting for confirmation (non-interactive / scripted use)

```bash
az group delete \
  --name learn-azure-cli \
  --yes \
  --no-wait
```

| Flag | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| `--yes` | Skips the confirmation prompt |
| `--no-wait` | Returns control to the terminal immediately; deletion continues in the background |

> Deleting a Resource Group removes **all resources inside it** — VMs, VNets, NSGs, storage accounts, public IPs, etc. Always confirm the correct Resource Group name before deleting.

---

## 9. DevOps Best Practices with Azure CLI

### Store scripts in a Git repository

```
repo/
└── azure-cli/
    ├── create-vm.sh
    ├── create-storage.sh
    └── delete-resources.sh
```

- Scripts in version control serve as both automation tools and documentation.
- Team members clone the repo, update names/regions as needed, and run the script.
- New joiners can provision environments without reading lengthy setup docs.

### Script structure recommendation

```bash
#!/bin/bash

# Variables — change these per project/environment
RESOURCE_GROUP="nike-sales-dev"
LOCATION="eastus"
VM_NAME="web-server-01"
VNET_NAME="nike-sales-vnet"
SUBNET_NAME="web-subnet"
STORAGE_ACCOUNT="nikesalesdevstorage"

# Step 1: Create Resource Group
az group create \
  --name $RESOURCE_GROUP \
  --location $LOCATION

# Step 2: Create VM + VNet
az vm create \
  --resource-group $RESOURCE_GROUP \
  --name $VM_NAME \
  --image Ubuntu2204 \
  --vnet-name $VNET_NAME \
  --subnet $SUBNET_NAME \
  --generate-ssh-keys

# Step 3: Create Storage Account
az storage account create \
  --name $STORAGE_ACCOUNT \
  --resource-group $RESOURCE_GROUP \
  --location $LOCATION \
  --sku Standard_LRS
```

> Parameterising scripts (using variables at the top) means the same script works for dev, staging, and production — just change the variable values.

---

## 10. Summary

| Task | Command |
|---|---|
| Check CLI version | `az version` |
| Login to Azure | `az login` |
| Create Resource Group | `az group create --name <name> --location <region>` |
| Create VM + VNet | `az vm create --resource-group <rg> --name <vm> --image Ubuntu2204 --vnet-name <vnet> --subnet <subnet> --generate-ssh-keys` |
| Create Storage Account | `az storage account create --name <name> --resource-group <rg> --location <region> --sku Standard_LRS` |
| Delete Resource Group | `az group delete --name <name> --yes --no-wait` |

**Dependency order when creating resources:**

```
Subscription (auto from az login)
    └── Resource Group
            ├── Virtual Network + Subnet
            │       └── Virtual Machine
            └── Storage Account
```

> Always create resources in dependency order. A VM cannot be created without a Resource Group, and it needs a VNet/Subnet available (or created at the same time with `az vm create`).

---
